[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Leak Detection, Garbage Collection, and Unmount Cleanups in React Native"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Memory leaks in React Native can degrade application performance, cause UI stuttering, and ultimately lead to crashes due to Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors. Understanding how the JavaScript engine's garbage collector interacts with native modules and React lifecycle unmount cleanups is crucial for building robust mobile applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Garbage Collection in React Native"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"React Native executes JavaScript on a separate thread (Hermes or JavaScriptCore). The garbage collector (GC) automatically frees memory by identifying objects that are no longer reachable from the root execution context. However, closures, lingering event listeners, active timers, and unresolved promises can maintain strong references to components, preventing the GC from reclaiming their memory."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Causes of Memory Leaks"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Uncleaned useEffect hooks containing active subscriptions or event listeners."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Forgetting to clear setInterval or setTimeout timers when a component unmounts."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Holding references to native modules or DOM nodes in global variables or static state."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Executing asynchronous operations (like network requests or state updates) on unmounted components."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Proper Unmount Cleanups"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To prevent memory leaks, every side effect initiated inside a useEffect hook must have a corresponding cleanup function returned. Below is an example of properly cleaning up an event listener and a timer."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { View, Text, BackHandler } from 'react-native';

const LeakyComponent = () => {
  const [counter, setCounter] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Setup interval timer
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      setCounter((prev) => prev + 1);
    }, 1000);

    // Setup hardware back press listener
    const backHandler = BackHandler.addEventListener('hardwareBackPress', () => {
      return true;
    });

    // Cleanup function executed on unmount
    return () => {
      clearInterval(timer);
      backHandler.remove();
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>Timer: {counter}</Text>
    </View>
  );
};

export default LeakyComponent;"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Preventing State Updates on Unmounted Components"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Asynchronous operations like fetch requests can finish long after a user has navigated away from a screen. Attempting to update state on an unmounted component triggers a React warning and retains memory. Using an abort controller or a tracking flag solves this issue."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { View, Text } from 'react-native';

const AsyncComponent = () => {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    let isMounted = true;

    fetch('https://api.example.com/data')
      .then((res) => res.json())
      .then((result) => {
        if (isMounted) {
          setData(result);
        }
      })
      .catch((err) => console.error(err));

    return () => {
      isMounted = false;
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>{data ? data.name : 'Loading...'}</Text>
    </View>
  );
};

export default AsyncComponent;"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Detecting Memory Leaks with Developer Tools"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Detecting memory leaks requires profiling tools. When using the Hermes engine, you can capture Hermes CPU and Heap profiles using Flipper or Chrome DevTools. Look for retained objects that increase in size over time despite navigating back and forth between screens. Additionally, React Native's LogBox will output warnings when state updates occur on unmounted components in development builds."}]
